WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.695 --> 00:00:18.176
Buck's ten-year-old sister, Sheila, stared at the Doggie Express van.

2
00:00:18.917 --> 00:00:20.677
She was short and stocky like Buck.

3
00:00:21.597 --> 00:00:24.458
"Your mom's van is so cool, Patrick!"

4
00:00:24.898 --> 00:00:25.478
Sheila said.

5
00:00:25.498 --> 00:00:27.319
"I love dogs.

6
00:00:27.399 --> 00:00:27.939
Don't you?"

7
00:00:28.959 --> 00:00:31.340
Patrick followed Sheila as she walked around the van.

8
00:00:34.108 --> 00:00:35.890
"Sure, I like dogs," he said.

9
00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:40.774
"My brother has a mutt named Buff that can catch a Frisbee and do lots of tricks.

10
00:00:41.754 --> 00:00:44.216
But Buff is scared to death of my cat, Slider.

11
00:00:44.857 --> 00:00:47.479
Slider is half his size and still rules our house!"

12
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:49.220
Sheila laughed.

13
00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:50.782
"I like her name."

14
00:00:51.782 --> 00:00:52.583
By now Mrs.

15
00:00:52.623 --> 00:00:56.406
Deboe was surrounded by other parents asking her about the dog-grooming business.

16
00:00:57.307 --> 00:00:59.629
His mother was small and wiry, like Patrick.

17
00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:03.432
All he could see were the red curls bouncing on top of her head.

18
00:01:04.924 --> 00:01:07.625
Suddenly a real howl came from the back of the van.

19
00:01:07.645 --> 00:01:13.867
"A-roo!" "My mother must already have a customer in there," said Patrick.

20
00:01:14.487 --> 00:01:16.607
"A-roo! A-roo!"

21
00:01:16.627 --> 00:01:20.028
"It sounds like an unhappy customer!"

22
00:01:20.388 --> 00:01:21.009
said Sheila.

23
00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:24.350
Patrick and Sheila peeked into the back window of the van.

24
00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:26.090
A dog was in a crate.

25
00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:28.031
"He looks sad.

26
00:01:28.431 --> 00:01:29.271
Can I pet him?"

27
00:01:29.711 --> 00:01:30.351
asked Sheila.

28
00:01:31.412 --> 00:01:32.772
"Sure," said Patrick.

29
00:01:33.312 --> 00:01:36.173
"Maybe you can cheer him up until my mom gets done talking to people."

30
00:01:37.014 --> 00:01:41.435
He opened the van's back door, and suddenly the door of the crate sprang open too.

31
00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:47.458
Patrick yelped as a cocker spaniel bounded out of the van.

32
00:01:47.478 --> 00:01:50.499
"Catch him!"

33
00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:52.420
yelled Daniel, running over to help.

34
00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.161
But the dog wasn't going anywhere.

35
00:01:55.781 --> 00:01:58.302
He danced maniacally around Sheila and Patrick.

36
00:02:01.604 --> 00:02:02.344
Sheila laughed.

37
00:02:02.752 --> 00:02:03.713
"He likes us!"

38
00:02:03.733 --> 00:02:05.414
"He sure does."

39
00:02:05.795 --> 00:02:08.977
Daniel picked up the pup and let Sheila pet him for a few minutes.

40
00:02:09.938 --> 00:02:13.701
Then he put the dog back in the crate and made sure the door was locked this time.

41
00:02:13.721 --> 00:02:19.106
"Thanks for capturing Otto, Daniel," said Mrs.

42
00:02:19.126 --> 00:02:21.387
Deboe as they all got in the van to head home.

43
00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:24.650
"You saved me from losing one of my first clients."

44
00:02:25.651 --> 00:02:26.391
Daniel grinned.

45
00:02:26.411 --> 00:02:28.633
"I am a catcher, you know."

46
00:02:29.634 --> 00:02:30.735
Mrs. Deboe laughed.

47
00:02:32.844 --> 00:02:34.906
"You can catch baseballs—and pets!"

48
00:02:36.347 --> 00:02:38.149
Everyone but Patrick laughed again.

49
00:02:41.332 --> 00:02:47.177
He stayed silent, staring out the window as his teammates barked at the Doggie Express van driving out

50
00:02:47.197 --> 00:02:50.340
of the parking lot.

51
00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.382
"What happened to my great day?"

52
00:02:53.783 --> 00:02:54.203
he thought.

53
00:02:55.184 --> 00:02:59.748
The first practice had been disappointing, and he had a pile of math homework waiting for him.

54
00:03:00.512 --> 00:03:06.376
Plus, no one in his family seemed to care that a new catcher—a really good new catcher—had joined

55
00:03:06.396 --> 00:03:06.956
the Dragons.

56
00:03:06.976 --> 00:03:12.580
"And, according to Daniel," Patrick thought, "I absolutely stink as a catcher!"

57
00:03:13.841 --> 00:03:15.062
During dinner Mrs.

58
00:03:15.102 --> 00:03:17.043
Deboe kept talking about her new business.

59
00:03:17.063 --> 00:03:20.866
"I got three jobs this afternoon just in the parking lot!

60
00:03:21.786 --> 00:03:24.688
I think Doggie Express is going to be a big success!"

61
00:03:24.708 --> 00:03:27.630
"We'll help when we can—right, boys?"

62
00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:29.031
Mr. Deboe said.

63
00:03:29.051 --> 00:03:31.365
"Woof!" Daniel grinned.

64
00:03:31.385 --> 00:03:34.587
"Sure," muttered Patrick.

65
00:03:35.307 --> 00:03:37.329
He was glad that his mom had a new business.

66
00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:39.810
But her dog van looked ridiculous.

67
00:03:40.731 --> 00:03:44.374
He didn't want to ride in it, especially not to and from baseball games.

68
00:03:44.994 --> 00:03:47.916
His teammates would never stop barking and howling at it.

69
00:03:49.857 --> 00:03:52.979
Half an hour later, Patrick and Daniel were washing dishes.

70
00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:57.142
"You want to go over some of the batters we'll be facing at the scrimmage next week?"

71
00:03:57.703 --> 00:03:59.504
asked Daniel when they were almost finished.

72
00:04:00.568 --> 00:04:01.848
"I'd like to sit in on that!"

73
00:04:02.189 --> 00:04:02.729
called Mr.

74
00:04:02.749 --> 00:04:03.849
Deboe from the living room.

75
00:04:04.669 --> 00:04:05.790
Mrs. Deboe frowned.

76
00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:08.910
"Patrick, don't you have math homework?"

77
00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:09.851
she asked.

78
00:04:10.751 --> 00:04:11.731
Patrick hesitated.

79
00:04:12.471 --> 00:04:17.553
He'd planned on starting his math homework after dinner, but a baseball strategy session sounded like a lot

80
00:04:17.573 --> 00:04:18.093
more fun.

81
00:04:18.113 --> 00:04:21.554
"Don't worry, Mom," he said quickly.

82
00:04:21.574 --> 00:04:23.114
"It's the weekend.

83
00:04:23.494 --> 00:04:24.895
I've got plenty of time for math.

84
00:04:25.455 --> 00:04:27.756
I can work on my homework later and all day tomorrow."

85
00:04:28.716 --> 00:04:30.581
"Okay," she said with a nod.

86
00:04:30.601 --> 00:04:33.202
"Just remember our discussion about math.

87
00:04:34.323 --> 00:04:36.284
You can't play baseball if your grades slip."

88
00:04:37.104 --> 00:04:38.865
"No problem," Patrick said.

89
00:04:38.885 --> 00:04:41.347
"I'm totally on top of the situation."

90
00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:44.428
Daniel sat at the computer while Patrick and Mr.

91
00:04:44.448 --> 00:04:46.009
Deboe pulled up chairs beside him.

92
00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:52.293
Daniel quickly found the roster for the team the Dragons were playing next week, the Swallows.

93
00:04:55.154 --> 00:04:56.915
Patrick scanned through the batters' names.

94
00:04:57.616 --> 00:05:01.390
In baseball, the catcher was the only player who could see the whole field.

95
00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:07.533
So it was his job to know where everyone was—his own players as well as any runners on

96
00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:12.576
base. It was a big advantage for a catcher to know about the players on the other team

97
00:05:13.016 --> 00:05:15.077
since the catcher was the one who called the pitches.

98
00:05:15.917 --> 00:05:17.518
Daniel pointed at Number 14.

99
00:05:17.538 --> 00:05:20.360
"This player crowds the base," he said.

100
00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:23.041
"Give him a pitch on the inside of the plate.

101
00:05:23.482 --> 00:05:25.843
He'll hit an outside pitch out of the park."

102
00:05:27.023 --> 00:05:28.716
"I remember him," said Mr.

103
00:05:28.736 --> 00:05:32.397
Deboe. "He hit that pop-up you caught in the first game last year."

104
00:05:33.618 --> 00:05:36.798
Patrick wasn't surprised that his father remembered a play from a year ago.

105
00:05:37.559 --> 00:05:42.000
Mr. Deboe practically lived on ballpark hotdogs and popcorn during baseball season.

106
00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:46.961
Patrick hoped that his father would soon be talking about Patrick's middle school plays too.

107
00:05:46.981 --> 00:05:51.483
"This one, Number 42, is a seventh-grader," continued Daniel.

108
00:05:51.503 --> 00:05:54.103
"He's a good hitter," Patrick murmured.

109
00:05:54.123 --> 00:05:56.064
"I remember him from Little League."

110
00:05:57.204 --> 00:05:58.525
Mr. Deboe leaned forward.

111
00:05:58.545 --> 00:06:00.666
"Think he can hit Dell's pitches?"

112
00:06:00.686 --> 00:06:06.089
"He may find that middle school pitchers are a lot tougher than what he's used to," said Daniel.

113
00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:08.531
It was after nine o'clock when Mrs.

114
00:06:08.571 --> 00:06:09.951
Deboe pointed to the clock.

115
00:06:10.612 --> 00:06:12.613
"Okay, Patrick, enough baseball.

116
00:06:13.033 --> 00:06:13.954
It's time for math."

117
00:06:15.354 --> 00:06:17.095
"Coming," Patrick murmured.

118
00:06:17.656 --> 00:06:20.577
He stood up a few minutes later and Daniel gave him a thumbs-up.

119
00:06:20.597 --> 00:06:24.780
"Work on learning the batters every night, and you'll be ready for the game."

120
00:06:25.704 --> 00:06:27.245
Mr. Deboe pointed to his head.

121
00:06:27.265 --> 00:06:29.845
"Half the game is in here," he said.

122
00:06:29.865 --> 00:06:32.266
"A catcher's got to be smart."

123
00:06:32.846 --> 00:06:33.726
He turned to Daniel.

124
00:06:33.746 --> 00:06:35.867
"Want to go out to the garage for a bit?"

125
00:06:36.647 --> 00:06:37.328
Daniel grinned.

126
00:06:37.748 --> 00:06:40.028
"Sure." Patrick wasn't surprised.

127
00:06:40.689 --> 00:06:42.509
Almost every night Daniel and Mr.

128
00:06:42.529 --> 00:06:44.410
Deboe worked on an old car that Mr.

129
00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:45.670
Deboe had bought last year.

130
00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.311
They both loved tinkering with engines.

131
00:06:49.711 --> 00:06:51.692
Patrick said good night and headed upstairs.

132
00:06:54.956 --> 00:06:58.458
He brushed his teeth, grabbed his math book, and plopped down on the bed.

133
00:07:00.339 --> 00:07:04.961
Maybe he needed to fill half his brain with baseball, but the other half had to be full

134
00:07:04.981 --> 00:07:05.481
of math.

135
00:07:06.281 --> 00:07:11.484
By 10:30 algebra formulas were swimming across the book pages like fish in the sea.

136
00:07:11.504 --> 00:07:15.686
"Plenty of time for math homework tomorrow," thought Patrick.

137
00:07:16.586 --> 00:07:18.527
A minute later he was sound asleep.
